# Kotlin/Android coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern
Kotlin/Android idiom. Applies only to `*.kt`/`*.kts` files — everything
not Kotlin-specific still comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Naming and structure

- `PascalCase` for classes, objects, and composables (a `@Composable`
  function that emits UI is named as a noun, `UserProfileCard`, not a verb);
  `camelCase` for functions, properties, and local variables; `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`
  only for `const val` compile-time constants.
- A `@Composable` function that returns a value instead of emitting UI
  (e.g. `rememberX`) still reads as a normal function name, not a noun —
  reserve the noun-style name for functions that actually emit UI.
- Keep a file's top-level surface small: one primary class/object per
  file named after it, with small private helpers colocated rather than
  scattered into a shared `Utils.kt`.
- Package names are all lowercase, no underscores, matching the directory
  layout (`com.example.feature.profile`, not `com.example.Feature.Profile`).

## Data classes vs regular classes

- Use `data class` for a type whose identity is its held values (a DTO, a
  UI state holder, a value object needing `equals`/`hashCode`/`copy`) —
  never a `data class` for something with real identity or mutable
  reference semantics (an Android `Activity`, a repository, a singleton
  manager).
- Prefer `copy()` with named parameters to produce a modified instance
  over building a fresh constructor call by hand; it keeps every unrelated
  field unchanged and the diff at a call site small when a new field is
  added later.
- A `data class` holding UI state should default every field to a sane
  initial value so `State()` (or `UiState()`) alone is a valid starting
  point.

## Null-safety idiom

- Prefer a safe call (`?.`) or the Elvis operator (`?:`) over `!!`; a
  forced-unwrap should be reserved for a value the type system cannot
  express as non-null but that a prior check (or the language, e.g. inside
  a `requireNotNull` block) has already proven non-null at that point —
  never as a shortcut past a compiler nullability warning.
- Use `let` for a null-checked scope over a nullable receiver
  (`value?.let { ... }`) when the block needs the unwrapped value once;
  avoid chaining several `?.let` calls where a single `if (value != null)`
  smart-cast reads more clearly.
- Prefer `requireNotNull(x) { "why x must be non-null here" }` over `x!!`
  at a boundary (constructor, public function entry) — it fails with a
  message that explains the invariant instead of a bare
  `NullPointerException`.
- Model "value or absent" with a nullable type or a sealed result type,
  not a sentinel value (`-1`, `""`) that callers have to remember to check
  for.

## Sealed classes/interfaces for state modeling

- Model a UI or domain state with a `sealed interface`/`sealed class`
  hierarchy (`Loading`, `Success(data)`, `Error(throwable)`) rather than a
  single class with several nullable fields and a separate `isLoading`
  boolean — the compiler then forces an exhaustive `when` at every call
  site, so a new state can't be silently unhandled.
- Prefer a `sealed interface` over `sealed class` when a variant needs no
  shared state or behavior; reach for `sealed class` only when the
  variants share a common property or method implementation.
- Keep a sealed hierarchy's variants in the same file (or a `sealed`-file
  group) as the parent — Kotlin does not require this, but scattering
  variants across files makes the exhaustiveness check hard to audit by
  reading.

## Extension functions

- Add an extension function to attach behavior to a type this module does
  not own (or to keep a small, focused helper out of a growing class) —
  not to route around giving a type a proper method when the type is
  already yours to edit.
- Keep an extension's receiver type narrow and its name unambiguous
  (`String.toDisplayDate()`, not a generic `Any.format()`); an extension
  with a vague name invites collisions and surprises at the call site.
- Avoid extension-function sprawl on core types (`String`, `Int`, `List`)
  for one-off, feature-specific logic — a feature-scoped extension on a
  feature-scoped type keeps the change's blast radius visible.
